diagnosis_text:
The fabric is operating normally; cross-rack L3 reachability is healthy. From pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) and back, pings succeed with 0% loss and low RTT (avg ~1–2.6 ms). The returned TTL=61 indicates traversal of three L3 hops, consistent with pc → leaf → spine → leaf → pc forwarding across the fabric.

Control-plane is up and distributing host subnets correctly. On leaf_router_0_0, 10.0.1.0/24 is installed via BGP and points to 172.16.0.4 on eth0. On leaf_router_0_1, 10.0.0.0/24 is likewise installed via BGP via 172.16.0.6 on eth0. Both leafs show BGP sessions established to their two spine neighbors with prefixes exchanged (PfxRcd=1, PfxSnt=2). The spines have all three BGP neighbors up (super-spine and both leafs) and are exchanging routes. This confirms the leaf_router_0_1 “top-of-rack switch” and the rest of the CLOS fabric are forwarding and routing as expected.

Mechanism: With correct BGP advertisements of 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 from the leafs, traffic between PCs is ECMP-routed via the spines, yielding stable, low-latency connectivity.

Localization: No faults detected on leaf_router_0_1 or elsewhere. All relevant interfaces and BGP processes are functioning.

Root cause: Not reproducible; no current misconfiguration or outage in the fabric.

Corrective fix: None required. If the issue recurs, capture timestamps with continuous ping/traceroute and check ARP/MTU on the PCs to isolate any intermittent host-side or transient control-plane events. === END ===